Common Golf Gambling Games
Nassau
Description: The most classic golf betting game, essentially three separate bets rolled into one.
Players: 2+, typically played in twosomes or foursomes
Rules:

Three separate bets: front nine, back nine, and overall 18 holes
Can be played as match play (holes won) or stroke play (total score)
Often includes "presses" - side bets that can be initiated when a player is down by a certain margin
Standard bet might be "2-2-2" meaning $2 for front nine, $2 for back nine, and $2 for the overall match

Skins
Description: Each hole is worth a "skin" (monetary value), with the value carrying over if the hole is tied.
Players: 2+
Rules:

Player with the lowest score on a hole wins that hole's skin
If multiple players tie for lowest score, the skin "carries over" to the next hole
Each skin is worth an agreed-upon amount, or progressive amounts
Games often end with dramatic final holes worth multiple skins

Wolf
Description: A rotating format where one player (the "Wolf") chooses partners after watching tee shots.
Players: 4 (ideal)
Rules:

Rotation changes each hole, determining who is the "Wolf"
Wolf watches other players' tee shots, then decides to partner with one player or play alone
If Wolf chooses a partner, they play as a team against the other two
If Wolf chooses to go "lone wolf," they play against all three other players
Points/money awarded based on which team wins the hole
Advanced versions include "Blind Wolf" where Wolf must choose before seeing tee shots

Vegas
Description: A team game with an unusual scoring system that can create dramatic swings.
Players: 4 (two teams of 2)
Rules:

Partners' scores are combined, but not added - they're paired together (e.g., scores of 5 and 4 become 54)
Lower combined score wins the difference times the stake
Modified versions exist where the higher score is always placed first
Double or triple payouts for large margins commonly used

Bingo Bango Bongo
Description: Points-based game rewarding different golf skills on each hole.
Players: 2+
Rules:

Three points available on each hole:

Bingo: First player to get their ball on the green
Bango: Player whose ball is closest to the pin once all balls are on the green
Bongo: First player to hole out


Points are worth predetermined amounts
Play proceeds in order of farthest from the hole, making strategy important

Stableford/Modified Stableford
Description: Points-based scoring system rewarding good play rather than penalizing bad holes.
Players: 2+
Rules:

Standard Stableford:

0 points for double bogey or worse
1 point for bogey
2 points for par
3 points for birdie
4 points for eagle
5 points for albatross


Modified Stableford (common variation):

-3 points for double bogey or worse
-1 point for bogey
0 points for par
2 points for birdie
5 points for eagle
8 points for albatross



Quota
Description: Players compete against their own predetermined "quota" of points.
Players: 2+
Rules:

Each player has a quota based on handicap
Points awarded using Stableford system (or similar)
Winner is the player who exceeds their quota by the most (or misses by the least)
Common formula: 36 minus handicap = quota

Nines (also called "9-points")
Description: A three-player game with 9 points distributed on each hole.
Players: 3
Rules:

5 points to lowest score, 3 points to middle score, 1 point to highest score
If two tie for low score: 4 points each, 1 point to high score
If two tie for high score: 5 points to low score, 2 points each to tied players
If all tie: 3 points each
Points are worth predetermined amounts

Garbage/Junk/Dots
Description: A collection of small side bets throughout the round.
Players: 2+
Rules:

Various achievements earn different payouts
Common categories include:

Birdies/Eagles
Greenies (closest to pin on par 3s)
Sandies (par after bunker shot)
Barkies (par after hitting a tree)
Poleys (making a putt longer than the flagstick)


Each achievement is worth a predetermined amount

Sixes (Hollywood or Round Robin)
Description: Partners rotate every six holes.
Players: 3
Rules:

Three different partnerships over 18 holes
Holes 1-6: Player A and B vs. C
Holes 7-12: Player A and C vs. B
Holes 13-18: Player B and C vs. A
Each six-hole segment is its own bet
Often played as match play for each segment